Architected rogue tower detection daemon leveraging Qualcomm DIAG protocol

Extract alongside the previous deploy scripts, then:
bash

bash deploy_tower_hunter.sh
adb shell su -c 'bash /sdcard/start_tower_hunt.sh'

What this module does and why

The layer below is the Qualcomm DIAG protocol on /dev/diag. Android's TelephonyManager gives you a heavily abstracted view — operator name, signal bars, connected/not. DIAG gives you the raw RRC (Radio Resource Control) and L1 messages the baseband actually sends and receives. Rogue towers can't sanitise what they transmit at that level.

Data sources, in depth order:
Source	What it exposes	Why it matters
/dev/diag 0x5008	GSM cipher mode per cell	A5/0 = no encryption = ROGUE flag, 60 points
/dev/diag 0x506C	Raw RR signalling messages	Cipher Mode Command captured verbatim
/dev/diag 0xB17E	LTE ML1 serving cell	eNB ID, EARFCN, RSRP, emergency-only flag
/dev/diag 0xB0C0	LTE RRC OTA messages	Raw SIBs, Security Mode Command
/dev/diag 0xB180/81	NAS EMM in/out	Attach type, security negotiation
AT+QENG="servingcell"	Serving + neighbour cell params	Cross-check, works without DIAG

Rogue indicators scored 0–100:

GSM: A5/0 no encryption (+60), A5/2 banned globally (+65), zero timing advance + strong signal (+25), empty BA list/no neighbours (+30), sudden LAC change (+25), ARFCN outside standard bands (+35).

LTE: Emergency-only attach (+50), unknown eNB with strong RSRP (+35), sudden TAC change (+30), eNB ID < 100/factory default (+25), EARFCN/band mismatch (+40), PCI collision on different eNB (+45).

Score ≥ 60 = ROGUE. Score ≥ 30 = SUSPECT.

Evidence chain: Every event is SHA-256(all fields + prev_hash), chained from a GENESIS block. The chain log is a separate file of just seq|prev|this triplets. Run chain_verify.py towers_*.log --report to get a JSON + plain text report of all rogues found, with chain integrity verification. Any tampering with the log breaks the chain at the modified line.
